#s
help  [<command>]
  Note: Here <command> is a valid command or a
        key word for group of commands. The key
        words for help are: cwsc,swsc,hldbac,lra
        sigc,intr,trig,sih,scc,bec,aux,all.

#t
Auxiliary Command
#p
Provides help on the specified command. If the
specified command is not unique, it lists all
commands with that prefix
    .eg., help vxi
will list all commands with prefix vxi.

#s
?  [<command>]
  Note: Here <command> is a valid command or a
        key word for group of commands. The key
        words for help are: cwsc,swsc,hldbac,lra
        sigc,intr,trig,sih,scc,bec,aux,all.
#t
Auxiliary Command
#p
Provides help on the specified command. If the
specified command is not unique, it lists all
commands with that prefix
    e.g., ? vxi
will list all commands with prefix vxi.

#s
wsrd [<la>,] <count>,<mode>
  <la>     logical address of servant device
  <count>  maximum number of bytes to transfer
  <mode>   mode of transfer (bit vector)
    Bit  Description
    ---- --------------
    0    Not DOR
         0 = abort if not DOR
         1 = poll till DOR
    1    END bit termination suppression
         0 = terminate on END bit
         1 = do not terminate on END bit
    2    LF character termination
         1 = terminate on LF bit
         0 = do not terminate on LF bit
    3    CR character termination
         1 = terminate on CR bit
         0 = do not terminate on CR bit
    4    EOS character termination
         1 = terminate on EOS bit
         0 = do not terminate on EOS bit
    8-15 EOS character (if enabled)
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Transfers data from a Message-Based device to 
the console using the VXIbus Byte Transfer 
Protocol.

#s
wsrdf [<la>,] <filename>,<count>,<mode>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <filename> file name
  <count>    number of bytes to transfer
  <mode>     mode of transfer (bit vector)
             (Same as in WSrd)
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Transfers data from a Message-Based device to
a file, using the VXIbus Byte Transfer Protocol.

#s
wswrt [<la>,] <string> [,<mode>]
  <la>      logical address of servant device
  <string>  buffer to send
  <mode>    mode of transfer (bit vector)
    Bit  Description
    ---- --------------
    0    Not DIR
         0 = abort if not DIR
         1 = poll till DIR
    1    END bit termination
         0 = clear END bit on the last byte
         1 = set END bit on the last byte 
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Transfers the specified string to a Message-
Based servant using VXIbus Byte Transfer
Protocol.

#s
wswrtf [<la>,] <filename> [,<count>]
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <filename> file name
  <count>    maximum number of bytes to
             transfer
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p 
Transfers data from the specified file to a
Message-Based servant using the VXIbus Byte
Transfer Protocol.

#s
wscmd [<la>,] <command>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <command>  Word Serial Command
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Send a Word Serial command to a Message-Based
servant device.

#s
wscmd? [<la>,] <command>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <command>  Word Serial query
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Sends a Word Serial query to a Message-Based
and gets the response.

#s
wsresp [<la>]
  <la>       logical address of servant device
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Gets the response to the previously sent
Word Serial query.

#s
wstrg [<la>]
  <la>       logical address of servant device
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Send the Word Serial Trigger command to 
a servant device.

#s
wsclr [<la>]
  <la>       logical address of servant device
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Sends the Word Serial Protocol command Clear to 
a Message-Based servant device.

#s
wsabort [<la>,] <abortop>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <abortop>  option to abort Word Serial
             operation
   1 = ForcedAbort: aborts WSwrt,WSrd and WStrg
   2 = UnSupCom: aborts WScmd, WSLcmd and WSEcmd
   3 = ForcedAbort: aborts WScmd, WSLcmd and 
       WSEcmd
   4 = ForcedAbort: aborts all Word Serial
       operations
   5 = Async ForcedAbort: aborts all Word Serial
       operations immediately.
       (See manual for details)
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Aborts the Commander Word Serial operation(s)
that is (are) in progress.
 
#s
wslcmd [<la>,] <command>,<respflag>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <command>  Longword Serial command
  <respflag> response flag
             0 = do not get the response
             1 = get the response
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Sends a Longword Serial command to a Message-
Based servant device and gets the response.

#s
wslresp [<la>]
  <la>       logical address of servant device
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Gets the response for the previously sent
Longword query.

#s
wsecmd [<la>,] <cmdext>,<command>,<respflag>
  <la>       logical address of servant device
  <cmdext>   upper 16 bits of 48-bit command
  <command>  lower 32 bits of 48-bit command
  <respflag> response flag
             0 = do not get the response
             1 = get the response
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Sends an Extended Longword Serial command to a 
Message-Based servant device and gets the
response.

#s
wssettmo <timeout>
  <timeout>     timeout period (in milliseconds)
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Sets the timeout period for all Word Serial
commands.

#s
wsgettmo 
#t
Commander Word Serial Command
#p
Gets the timeout period set for all Word Serial
commands.

#s
wssenable 
#t
Servant Word Serial Command
#p
Enables all Servant Word Serial Protocol
commands.

#s
wssdisable 
#t
Servant Word Serial Command
#p
Disables all Servant Word Serial Protocol
commands.

#s
wssrd  <count>,<mode>
  <count> maximum number of bytes to transfer
  <mode>  mode of transfer (bit vector)
    Bit  Description
    ---- --------------
    0    0 = do not send DIR signal to Commander
         1 = send DIR signal to Commander
#t
Servant Word Serial Command
#p
Receives data from a Message-Based commander
using the VXIbus Byte Transfer Protocol.
 
#s
wsswrt  <string>,<mode>
  <string> buffer to send
  <mode>   mode of transfer (bit vector)
    Bit  Description
    ---- --------------
    0    0 = do not send DOR signal to Commander
         1 = send DOR signal to Commander
    1    0 = do not send END with the last byte
         1 = send END with the last byte
#t
Servant Word Serial Command
#p
Transfers data to a Message-Based commander
using the VXIbus Byte Transfer Protocol.

#s
wssabort  <abortop>
  <abortop>  option to abort Servant Word Serial
             operation
   Bit  Description
   ---- --------------
    0   Abort WSSwrt
    1   Abort WSSrd
    2   Abort WSSsendResp
   15   Initialize Word Serial Servant hardware.
        This includes aborting all Word Serial
        Servant operations, clearing all errors,
        removing all pending Word Serial servant
        interrupts and disabling the interrupts.
#t
Servant Word Serial Command
#p
Aborts Servant Word Serial operations

#s
vxiin <accessparms>,<address>,<width>
  <accessparms> (bits 0-1) VXI address space
                  1 = A16, 2 = A24, 3 = A32
                (bits 2-4) access privilege
                  0 = non-priv data,  1 = priv
                  2 = non-priv prog,  3 = priv
                  4 = non-priv block, 5 = priv
                (bit 7) byte order
                  0 = Motorola, 1 = Intel
                (bits 5-6) and (bits 8-15)
                  0
  <address>     VXI address 
  <width>       1 = byte,2 = word,4 = longword 
#t
High-Level VXIbus Access Commands
#p
Reads a single byte, word, or longword from the
specified address in the specified address space
with the specified access parameters.

#s
vxiout <accessparms>,<address>,<width>,<value>
  <accessparms> (bits 0-1) VXI address space
                  1 = A16, 2 = A24, 3 = A32
                (bits 2-4) access privilege
                  0 = non-priv data,  1 = priv
                  2 = non-priv prog,  3 = priv
                  4 = non-priv block, 5 = priv
                (bit 7) byte order
                  0 = Motorola, 1 = Intel
                (bits 5-6) and (bits 8-15)
                  0
  <address>     VXI address 
  <width>       1 = byte,2 = word,4 = longword 
  <value>       data to write
#t
High-Level VXIbus Access Commands
#p
Writes a single byte, word, or longword to the 
specified address in the specified address 
space with the specified access parameters.

#s
vxiinreg [<la>,] <reg>
  <la>       logical address 
  <reg>      offset within VXI logical address
             registers
#t
High-Level VXIbus Access Commands
#p
Reads a single word from the specified register
offset on the specified logical address.

#s
vxioutreg [<la>,] <reg>,<value>
  <la>       logical address 
  <reg>      offset within VXI logical address 
             registers
  <value>    value to write
#t
High-Level VXIbus Access Commands
#p
Writes a single word to the specified register
offset on the specified logical address.

#s
vximove <srcparms>,<srcaddr>,<destparms>,
        <destaddr>,<length>,<width>
  <srcparms>    (bits 0-1) VXI address space
 and <destparms>     1 = A16, 2 = A24, 3 = A32
                (bits 2-4) access privilege
                  0 = non-priv data,  1 = priv
                  2 = non-priv prog,  3 = priv
                  4 = non-priv block, 5 = priv
                (bit 7) byte order
                  0 = Motorola, 1 = Intel
                (bits 5-6) and (bits 8-15)
                  0
  <srcaddr> and <destaddr>       address 
  <length>       number of elements to transfer
  <width>        1 = byte,2 = word,4 = longword 
#t
High-Level VXIbus Access Commands
#p
Moves a block of bytes, words, or longwords 
from a source location in any address space
to a destination in any address space with
the specified access parameters.

#s
getmyla 
#t
Local Resource Access Commands
#p
Gets the logical address of the local VXI 
device.

#s
vxiinlr <reg>,<width>
  <reg>    offset within VXI logical address 
           registers
  <width>  1 = byte,2 = word,4 = longword 
#t
Local Resource Access Commands
#p
Reads a single byte, word, or longword from
the specified register on the local VXI device.

#s
vxioutlr <reg>,<width>,<value>
  <reg>     offset within VXI logical address 
            registers
  <width>   1 = byte,2 = word,4 = longword 
  <value>   value to write
#t
Local Resource Access Commands
#p
Writes a single byte, word, or longword from
the specified register on the local VXI device.

#s
setmodid <enable>,<modid>
  <enable>     1 = set MODID enable bit
               0 = clear MODID enable bit
  <modid>      bit vector for bits 0-12 
               corresponding to slots 0-12
#t
Local Resource Access Commands
#p
Controls the MODID lines of the VXIbus
backplane.

#s
readmodid  
#t
Local Resource Access Commands
#p
Read the MODID lines of the VXIbus backplane.

#s
routesignal [<la>,] <modemask>
  <la>       logical address to set handler for
  <modemask> a bit vector that specifies whether
             each type of signal is enqueued or 
             handled by the signal handler. A
             zero in any bit position causes
             signals of the associated type to 
             be queued.  All other signals are
             handled by the signal handler.
    (Please see the manual for bit positions)
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Selects whether each type of signal is enqueued
on a software queue or handled by the signal
handler.
 
#s
enablesignalint
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Sensitizes VIC to receive all signals.

#s
disablesignalint
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Desensitizes VIC to not receive signals.

#s
signaldeq  [<la>,] <signalmask>
  <la>       logical address of signal to 
             dequeue for (-1 = any)
  <signalmask> Bit Vector
               (0xffff = any)
    (Please see the manual for bit positions)
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Gets a signal specified by the signalmask 
for the specified logical address from 
the software signal queue.

#s
signalenq <signal>
  <signal>       signal to enqueue at the
                 end of the queue
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Puts the specified signal on the tail of the
global software signal queue.

#s
signaljam <signal>
  <signal>       signal to jam in the front
                 of the queue
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Puts the signal on the head of the global
software signal queue.

#s
waitforsignal [<la>,] <signalmask>,<timeout>
  <la>       logical address of device
             to wait on (-1 = any)
  <signalmask> Bit Vector
               (0xffff = any)
    (Please see the manual for bit positions)
  <timeout>  time period to wait 
             (in milliseconds)
#t
VXI Signal Commands
#p
Waits for one of the specified signals to be
received.

#s
routevxiint  <controller>,<sroute>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1.
  <sroute>     bit vector of VXI interrupt
               levels to route
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to VXI
               interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = handle as a VXI signal
               0 = handle as a VXI interrupt
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Selects whether VXI interrupts are handled
as VXI signals or routed to the VXI interrupt
handler for the specified controller

#s
enablevxitosignalint <controller>,<levels>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <levels>     bit vector of VXI interrupt 
               levels to enable
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to 
               VXI interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = enable for the level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Sensitizes the specified controller to the specified 
VXI interrupt levels being processed as VXI signals 

#s
disablevxitosignalint <controller>,<levels>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <levels>     bit vector of VXI interrupt 
               levels to disable
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to 
               VXI interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = disable for the level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Desensitizes the specified controller to the specified 
VXI interrupt levels being processed as VXI signals 
 
#s
enablevxiint <controller>,<levels>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <levels>     bit vector of VXI interrupt 
               levels to enable
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to 
               VXI interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = enable for the level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Sensitizes the specified controller to the 
specified VXI interrupt levels being processed 
as VXI/VME interrupt (not as VXI signal) 

#s
disablevxiint <controller>,<levels>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <levels>     bit vector of VXI interrupt 
               levels to disable
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to 
               VXI interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = disable for the level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Desensitizes the specified controller to the 
specified VXI interrupt levels being processed 
as VXI/VME interrupt (not as VXI signal)

#s
vxiintacknowledgemode <controller>,<modes>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <modes>      bit vector of VXI interrupt 
               levels to set to RORA or ROAK
               interrupt acknowledge mode. 
               Bits 6 to 0 correspond to 
               VXI interrupt levels 7 to 1.
               1 = set to RORA VXI int 
               0 = set to ROAK VXI int 
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Specifies whether the VXI interrupt acknowledge
cycle for the specified controller for the 
specified levels should be handled as Release On
Acknowledge (ROAK) or as Release On Register 
Access (RORA).

#s
assertvxiint <controller>,<level>,<statusId>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <level>      interrupt level
  <statusId>   status/ID to present during
               IACK cycle
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p 
Asserts an interrupt line in a particular
controller.

#s
deassertvxiint <controller>,<level>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <level>      interrupt level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Deasserts an interrupt line in the specified
controller.

#s
acknowledgevxiint <controller>,<level>
  <controller> logical address of extended
               controller or embedded controller
               to configure or -1
  <level>      interrupt level
#t
VXI Interrupt Commands
#p
Performs an IACK cycle on the VXIbus in the
specified controller for the specified level.

